course: dictionary
lesson: 3967
title: Fuss: worry, complaining and loving attention
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Fuss is a word with more than one feeling. «When your parent says it is time to go, you finish quickly, say goodbye and go without a fuss.» Without a fuss means calmly, with no complaining or making a scene. Look at the words around it: finish quickly and go tell you the story is about leaving nicely.
explain: Fuss can also be a verb. «Ming's yéye and nǎinai live with his family, and to them you are a guest child to be fussed over.» Fussed over is a clue: the grandparents are giving a guest lots of attention. To fuss can mean to worry about small things, but here it is kind.
explain: The same word can be trouble or love. «Make a fuss» means to complain or get upset. «Make a fuss of someone» means to give them lots of loving attention. Ask yourself: is someone upset, or is someone being looked after? The clues tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Sometimes the meaning is hidden. A friend who is hurt may say «It's just a scratch» or «I'm fine, honestly». They may be embarrassed and not want a fuss. The words say one thing, but the feeling under them is different, so read the whole situation before you decide.
fact: Fuss is a noun and a verb: a fuss, to fuss, fusses, fussed, fussing.
fact: As a noun, fuss means a lot of worry, excitement or complaining, often about something small.
fact: As a verb, to fuss means to worry or pay too much attention to small things.
fact: «Make a fuss of someone» means to give them lots of loving attention.
fact: Useful phrases: «kick up a fuss», «what's all the fuss about?», «fuss over», «no fuss».
exercise: «When your parent says it is time to go, you finish quickly, say goodbye and go without a fuss.» What does without a fuss mean here? | Loudly, so everyone can hear you | Slowly, while you argue about leaving | *Calmly, with no complaining or making a scene
exercise: To the grandparents, a guest child is to be fussed over. What does fussed over mean? | Shouted at for being late | *Given lots of loving attention | Ignored and left alone | Forgotten at the door
exercise: A friend is hurt and says «It's just a scratch». Why might they say that? | They are hungry and want food | They have just won a prize | *They are embarrassed and don't want a fuss | They want everyone to make a big fuss of them
test: Which phrase means to give someone lots of loving attention? | kick up a fuss | *make a fuss of someone | no fuss | what's all the fuss about?
test: Which sentence uses fuss as a verb meaning to worry about small things? | *Mum will fuss about every little crumb on the floor | Grandad gave us a fuss-free ride home | We left the party with no fuss | There was a fuss at the school gate this morning
exam: Which meaning fits «kick up a fuss»? | To give someone lots of loving attention | To leave quietly without saying goodbye | *To complain loudly or make a lot of trouble about something | To be very hungry after a long walk
